==Russian==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|ru|cu|съзьдати||to build, to erect}} (1sg. {{m|cu|съзиждѫ}}). Not originally a compound of {{m|ru|дать}}, but later borrowed its conjugation from that verb. Compare also the corresponding older imperfective {{m|ru|созида́ть}}, with the same relationship as imperfective {{m|ru|-бира́ть}} to {{m|ru|брать}}. Synchronically derivable as {{m|ru|со-}} + {{m|ru||-здать}}, found only in this verb and derived from {{cog|orv|зьдати}} (1sg. {{m|orv|зижу}}). Cognate with {{cog|cu|зьдати||to build, to erect}} (1sg. {{m|cu|зиждѫ}}), {{cog|bg|зи́дам||to build, to construct}}, {{m|bg|зид||wall}}, {{cog|sh|зи́дати||to construct (from stones)}} (1sg. {{m|sh|зи̑да̄м}}), {{m|sh|зи̑д||stone wall}} (gen. sg. {{m|sh|зи́ду}}), {{cog|sl|zídati||to build a wall}}, {{m|sl|zȋd||wall}}, {{cog|cs|zeď ||stone wall}} (gen. sg. {{m|cs|zdi}}), {{cog|pl|zdun||potter}}.

Per Vasmer, further cognate with {{cog|lt|žiẽsti||to mold}} (1sg. {{m|lt|žiedžiù}}, 1sg. past {{m|lt|žiedžiaũ}}), {{cog|lv|zìest||to smear with clay}}, {{cog|prg|seydis||wall}} and more distantly (with different vowels) with {{cog|lt|žaĩdas||oven}}, {{m|lt|židinỹs||hearth}}, {{cog|got|𐌳𐌴𐌹𐌲𐌰𐌽|t=to knead}}, {{cog|sa|देघ्दि|tr=déghdi|t=to plaster}}, {{m|sa|देही|tr=dehī́|t=shaft, dam, mound|g=f}}, {{cog|peo|tr=didā|||strengthening}} {{cog|grc|τεῖχος||stone wall}}, {{m|grc|τοῖχος||wall}}, {{cog|la|fingō||to shape, to knead}}, {{cog|osc|féihúss||walls {{i|acc. pl.}}}} {{cog|txh|-δίζος}}. The Balto-Slavic forms come from {{cog|ine-bsl-pro|*ǵʰeydʰ-}}, metathesis of {{cog|ine-pro|*dʰeyǵʰ-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA|созда́ть}}
